---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatStrip from '../components/khensu/StatStrip.astro';
---

<Base title="Architecture" description="The parts of khensu.ñü and what each one does.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
  <h1>How the pieces fit</h1>
  <StatStrip
    items={[
      { n: 6, label: 'Parts, from the app to suñůlink.shield' },
      { n: 0, label: 'Company servers in the middle' },
      { n: 5, label: 'Mix network hops for every message' },
      { n: 2, label: 'Relays per call, run by different operators' },
    ]}
  />
  <p class="lede">Your devices hold your data; nodes run by people carry it without reading it or seeing who it’s for.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>App (<code>nu.khensu.ptah</code>)</td><td>SwiftUI app for iPhone, iPad and Mac on iOS, iPadOS and macOS 26, built only from Apple’s stock Liquid Glass controls. Holds your keys, messages and private on-device AI.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Core library</td><td>Rust: the post-quantum MLS suite, credentials, mix network packets and the on-device search index, shared with the app as a Swift framework.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Node (<code>nu.khensu.tatenan</code>)</td><td>Runs on a Raspberry Pi 5, a Mac, a laptop or desktop in the background, or an old iPhone. Mixes and forwards traffic, keeps encrypted mailboxes, relays calls and earns for the work.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Chain</td><td>A post-quantum ledger for the network’s token and shielded payments, where amounts are hidden and leave no lasting record.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Services</td><td>A public key transparency log so anyone can check that a handle’s keys haven’t been swapped, a credential issuer, and a push gateway that wakes phones without learning anything.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>suñůlink.shield</td><td>An optional post-quantum mesh transceiver in a titanium or brass shell, which turns your iPhone into part of the network.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Handles</h2>
  <p>Everyone gets a permanent handle at <code>name.khensu.nu</code>, resolved from the key transparency log over the mix network, so looking someone up reveals nothing.</p>

  <h2>Calls</h2>
  <p>Messages go through the mix network. Live calls can’t take a mixer’s delays, so they use two relays run by different operators, and the route changes as you talk.</p>

  <h2>Full specifications</h2>
  <p>The protocol and threat model specifications are published alongside the code, once each component’s design review is finished.</p>
</Base>
